{"question": "A 13-year-old boy is brought to his pediatrician due to a left breast lump under his nipple. He noticed it last month and felt that it has increased slightly in size. It is tender to touch but has no overlying skin changes. There is no breast discharge. The patient has cryptorchidism as an infant and underwent a successful orchiopexy. In addition, he was recently diagnosed with ADHD and is currently on methylphenidate with improvement in his symptoms. He has a family history of type I diabetes in his father and breast fibroadenoma in his mother. On exam, the patient is at the 82nd percentile for height, 79th percentile for weight, and 80th percentile for BMI. He has tanner IV pubic hair. Testicular volume is 7 mL on each side, and both testes are smooth and freely mobile. Breast exam shows a normal right breast and a 3-centimeter round, firm, and slightly tender discrete mass under the left nipple. Which of the following is the most likely etiology of this patient\u2019s condition?", "options": {"A": "Klinefelter syndrome", "B": "Glandular tissue enlargement", "C": "Lipomastia (pseudogynecomastia)", "D": "Testicular cancer", "E": "Medication side effect"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Adolescent Medicine", "Endocrinology", "Urology", "Oncology"], "option_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Surgery"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The medical scenario presented describes a 13-year-old boy with a left breast lump, which is a condition known as gynecomastia. Gynecomastia is the enlargement of breast tissue in males, which can be due to various causes, including hormonal imbalances, medications, and underlying medical conditions. ### Interpretation of the Condition\n\n1. **Age and Presentation**: The patient is a 13-year-old boy, which is a common age for gynecomastia to occur, often due to normal pubertal changes. However, the presence of a discrete, firm, and tender mass is noteworthy and may suggest a more spe